{"id":"b687bc8b-c820-4631-9691-cb4641fbde24","object":"chat.completion","created":1785033544,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n['parent']\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        all_children_removed = all(cid in removed for cid in children[nid])\\n        if not all_children_removed:\\n            continue\\n        removed.add(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed])\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removal_order, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) based on the task description and provided fixtures. The task \"SE-PE-01\" is about grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree (nodes with parent, id, symbol, depth, removable, utility). The objective is to remove removable nodes while preserving required symbols and interestingness (likely meaning the tree remains valid with respect to some grammar? But the description says \"preserving an interestingness oracle\" – but we don't have an oracle; we only have required_symbols. The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to decide which nodes to remove.\n\nThe algorithm: Consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes. For nodes at same depth, use node ID as tie break. So we process nodes in order of depth descending, then by ID ascending? \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Typically, we process by depth descending, and for equal depth, sort ascending by ID. We need to decide whether to remove a node. Conditions for removal: node.removable must be True, node is not root, node's symbol is not in required_symbols, and after removal, all required symbols still exist somewhere in the tree (i.e., there is at least one node with that symbol that is not being removed, or if the node to remove has required symbol? Actually \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" means we cannot remove a node whose symbol is required? The required_symbols list includes symbols, not node IDs. So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it. Also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" – meaning if a node has children that are retained (not removed), we cannot remove that node (because removing it would remove those children? Actually if we remove a node, its children would be orphaned; but the description says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" – that likely means if we decide to retain a child, we cannot remove the parent. But we are deciding removal order; we need to consider that removing a node also removes all its descendants unless they are reattached? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but we are not doing replacement; only deletion. So removing a node removes its entire subtree. So we need to ensure that before removing a node, we have already processed its children (deeper nodes) and possibly removed some of them. But if we remove a parent, all its children are removed regardless. So the condition \"node with retained children\" might mean if any child is retained (not removed), then the parent cannot be removed because that would remove those children. Alternatively, it could mean we cannot remove a node if it still has children that we plan to keep. Since we are processing from leaves upward, we can decide to remove a node only if all its children are already removed or will be removed? The instruction: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any children that are retained (i.e., not removed), we cannot remove that node. That means removal must happen bottom-up: we remove leaves first, then upwards, but only if all children are removed. So algorithm: Build a tree structure. Determine the set of nodes to remove. Process nodes in order of depth descending, then ID ascending. For each node, check: if it is removable, not root, its symbol not in required_symbols, and all its children (if any) are already marked as removed (or none exist), then we can tentatively remove it, but also need to check that all required symbols are still present after removal. Since we are removing a subtree, we need to ensure that each required symbol appears in at least one node not removed. But the node itself might have a required symbol, but we already checked it's not required symbol. So we need to check the whole tree. Since we are processing bottom-up, we can keep a set of remaining symbols after removals. Initially all symbols are present. When we remove a node, we decrement the count for the symbol of that node and all its descendants. But since we process bottom-up and only remove if all children are removed, the children's symbols have already been removed. So we can maintain a mapping from symbol to count of nodes that are still present. Initially count each symbol. When we remove a node, we subtract 1 for its symbol, and also for each descendant (but if we process bottom-up, descendants are already removed, so we only need to handle the node's own symbol). However, there might be multiple nodes with the same symbol; we only need at least one remaining for each required symbol.\n\nThus algorithm:\n1. Parse nodes into dict by id. Build children list for each parent.\n2. Determine required_symbols set.\n3. Compute initial symbol counts.\n4. Process nodes sorted by depth descending, then id ascending.\n5. For each node:\n   - If not removable, skip.\n   - If id == \"root\" (or parent is null) skip.\n   - If node.symbol in required_symbols: skip.\n   - Check that for every child of node, the child is already removed (i.e., in removed set). Since we process bottom-up, children should have been processed earlier (depth greater, or same depth? But same depth nodes cannot be parent-child because parent has lower depth. So children have higher depth, so they are processed before parent. So we can check if all children are in removed_set.\n   - Also check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to become absent. Since we only remove nodes that are not required symbols themselves, and we only remove when all children are already removed, the only symbol that could be lost is the node's own symbol. So we need to check that after removing this node, for this symbol, there is at least one other node with that symbol still present (non-removed). So if node.symbol count is 1 and we remove it, then required symbol absence if that symbol is required. But we already skip required symbols, so node.symbol is not required. But there might be other non-required symbols? The condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" only concerns required symbols. So if node.symbol is not required, removal doesn't affect required symbols. So the check is automatically satisfied. However, there is a subtlety: what if a descendant has a required symbol? But since we only remove a node when all its children are already removed, the descendant required symbols would have been removed already only if those descendant nodes were themselves removable. But if a descendant has a required symbol, it cannot be removed (since we skip required symbols). So a node with a descendant that is required cannot be removed because that child would not be removed, thus the condition \"node with retained children\" would prevent removal. So indeed the check holds.\n\nThus the main condition is: removable, not root, symbol not required, and all children already removed.\n\nBut we also must consider \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" As argued, it's automatically true if we respect previous conditions. However, there might be case where a node has a descendant that is required but still marked as removed? No, because required nodes are not removed. So they are retained, so the parent cannot be removed because it has a retained child. So safe.\n\nAfter processing, we have a set of removed node IDs. Then we need to return removed_node_ids (list) in the order they were removed? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (the order we processed and decided to remove). retained_node_ids should be the IDs of nodes not removed, sorted. Also need to include root and required symbols regardless.\n\nFor the given fixtures, we can test the logic. But we don't have the expected output. We'll implement as described.\n\nEdge cases: Some nodes may have no children; then condition \"all children removed\" is vacuously true.\n\nAlso need to handle if a node is removable but its parent is not removed? That's fine; removal is independent.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build adjacency list children: for each node, add child id to parent's list.\n- Build map id->node.\n- Remove set.\n- Removal_order list.\n- Process sorted nodes by (-depth, id). Since we want descending depth, we can sort by depth descending: key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']).\n- For each node in that order:\n   if node['id'] in removed_set: continue (already removed as child of another removed node? Actually we process all nodes, but if a node is removed as part of ancestor removal, it will be in removed_set from ancestor's removal? We don't remove ancestors yet; we only remove nodes individually. If we decide to remove a node, we add it to removed_set. But if later we decide to remove its parent, we must remove parent as well. However, our algorithm removes leaf nodes first, then parents. But if a parent is removed, all its children are removed as well. But if we already removed a child individually, that's fine. But we need to ensure that when we remove a parent, we also ensure that no child is retained. Since we process bottom-up, by the time we consider parent, all children have been processed. If any child was not removed (e.g., because it was not removable or required), then parent cannot be removed. So we don't need to remove children again. So we only add the node itself to removed_set when we remove it. But we should also mark its descendants as removed? Actually the semantics of tree pruning: if we remove a node, the entire subtree is gone. So we should not consider those descendant nodes as separate removals because they are implicitly removed. However, the requirement says \"Return the actual removal order\" – likely they want the order in which nodes are explicitly removed. If we remove a parent, we don't need to list children as separate removals. But the algorithm processes deeper nodes first; if we remove a child first, then later remove parent, the child is already removed. That's fine. But if we decide to skip a child (e.g., required), then parent cannot be removed. So the order of removals will be some leaves then possibly internal nodes. So we should only add the node when we decide to remove it, and we don't add its children again. However, we must ensure that when we check \"all children already removed\", we include children that are removed via parent removal? That cannot happen because parent is processed after children. So children are either removed individually or not at all. So the condition is correct.\n\nThus implement.\n\nNow output format: {\"removed_node_ids\": list of IDs in removal order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted list of IDs not in removed_set}.\n\nAlso need to handle infeasible cases? The description says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". Infeasibility might occur if required_symbols cannot be satisfied? But given the tree always has root with symbol \"root\" which is likely required. So not.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that expects case to have keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Returns dict.\n\nNow produce the implementation as a string, embedded in {\"implementation\": \"...\"}.\n\nWe need to be careful with JSON escaping. Write the Python source, then escape backslashes, quotes, newlines.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n['parent']\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    # root has parent null\n    removed = set()\n    removal_order = []\n    # sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        # check conditions\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if n['parent'] is None: # root\n            continue\n        if n['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check all children are removed\n        all_children_removed = all(cid in removed for cid in children[nid])\n        if not all_children_removed:\n            continue\n        # tentatively remove\n        # Since we have ensured symbol not required and children removed, required symbols remain.\n        removed.add(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    # retained nodes are those not removed\n    retained = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed])\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removal_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained}\n\nBut we should also consider that when we remove a node, its parent might later be considered. That's fine.\n\nPotential issue: The condition \"Never remove a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its direct children are retained. However, our check ensures that all children are removed. But what if some children are not removable? Then they will remain, so parent cannot be removed. That's correct. But what about grandchildren? Since we process bottom-up, grandchildren are either removed or retained; if they are retained, then the child (parent of grandchild) cannot be removed because it has a retained child. Then the grandparent cannot be removed because the child is retained. So propagation works.\n\nThus implementation is good.\n\nNow produce JSON with implementation key. Note the task says \"The evaluator invokes solve(case) once for each fixture row. The argument is exactly the inner object stored in that row's payload field.\" So case is the payload dict. The function should not rely on any other fields.\n\nWe must ensure we don't mutate case. We are only reading.\n\nEdge: If a node has id that is not listed? Not.\n\nNow write the response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3302,"total_tokens":5474,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2992},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}